WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.905
Secure firmware is built from repeatable
engineering habits.

2
00:00:03.985 --> 00:00:06.361
Start by defining device trust boundaries.

3
00:00:06.441 --> 00:00:08.561
What code runs before the application?

4
00:00:08.641 --> 00:00:11.082
What interfaces bring input into the
device?

5
00:00:11.162 --> 00:00:12.319
What state is stored?

6
00:00:12.399 --> 00:00:14.197
Which code can control hardware?

7
00:00:14.277 --> 00:00:16.204
Which paths have higher authority?

8
00:00:16.284 --> 00:00:18.469
Clear boundaries make review possible.

9
00:00:18.549 --> 00:00:20.604
Validate inputs and protect secrets.

10
00:00:20.684 --> 00:00:25.374
Peripheral data, communication messages,
configuration records, update metadata,

11
00:00:25.454 --> 00:00:29.372
and service commands should be checked
before they influence behavior.

12
00:00:29.452 --> 00:00:33.884
Keys, certificates, identifiers, and
credentials should have defined purpose,

13
00:00:33.964 --> 00:00:37.433
storage assumptions, logging rules, and
lifecycle ownership.

14
00:00:37.513 --> 00:00:39.119
Keep C behavior predictable.

15
00:00:39.199 --> 00:00:42.604
Review fixed buffers, stack usage, heap
policy, memory pools,

16
00:00:42.684 --> 00:00:46.538
object lifetime, pointer ownership,
integer sizes, parser behavior,

17
00:00:46.618 --> 00:00:49.316
interrupt interactions, and memory-mapped
I/O.

18
00:00:49.396 --> 00:00:54.021
Firmware security improves when memory
rules are visible and tested under realistic

19
00:00:54.101 --> 00:00:54.872
constraints.

20
00:00:54.952 --> 00:00:57.586
Verify startup, updates, and failure
behavior.

21
00:00:57.666 --> 00:01:00.492
Ask what the device checks before running
firmware,

22
00:01:00.572 --> 00:01:03.592
how update integrity and compatibility are
evaluated,

23
00:01:03.672 --> 00:01:07.141
what happens during power loss, how
rollback or recovery works,

24
00:01:07.221 --> 00:01:09.019
and how invalid state is handled.

25
00:01:09.099 --> 00:01:11.926
The negative paths matter as much as the
normal path.

26
00:01:12.006 --> 00:01:15.089
Finally, review production hardening and
diagnostics.

27
00:01:15.169 --> 00:01:17.160
What debug features remain enabled?

28
00:01:17.240 --> 00:01:19.810
What commands are available in release
builds?

29
00:01:19.890 --> 00:01:21.175
What logs are retained?

30
00:01:21.255 --> 00:01:23.824
What happens on reset, fault, or failed
update?

31
00:01:23.904 --> 00:01:27.630
Are these paths tested on hardware under
realistic timing, storage,

32
00:01:27.710 --> 00:01:28.931
and power conditions?

33
00:01:29.011 --> 00:01:33.700
Those questions help firmware teams ship
devices that can be maintained responsibly.
